er states we expect all countries to  provide accurate data as much as  possible  because World Health Organization is  operating under international health  regulations and this document is being  agreed by 194 member states so basically  member states themselves countries set  set the rules and we expect them to go  by those rules China has been a basic  new virus obviously at the beginning of  the of the new new outbreak of a new  disease you will be testing to people  who present with symptoms so it would be  difficult to expect that there is a  accurate number and we should really  look across the board because countries  have completely different testing  policies right now and some countries  have been so much overwhelmed by the  number of patients that the reporting  was very difficult so basically what we  were saying about China was that today  they did share genetic sequence of the  virus quickly and that helped to hold  countries to develop the tests and  basically to do the testing they also  did introduce very strong public health  measures that how them gets their  numbers down we trust the figures coming  out of China many say we are on can we  trust them I think we have to look  across the board so so what figures are  really accurate if you if some countries  for example do not test that population  so how can we have really accurate  numbers if some countries have you know  like Italy was dealing in so many cases  inside hospitals how they can generate  really data because the priority is to  save lives so I think we should every  day that we get is is not it is perfect  let's put it that way data is an  information that helps us make best  possible public health a guidance and  this is what are you okay problems of  course I if the data can't be relied on  but thank you very much for that  interview it's much appreciated we're  out and sadly  you

